WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
What benchmarks can’t tell you.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:13.187
Even a clean, uncontaminated benchmark measures a narrow thing: whether an agent, in a given harness, produces a change that passes someone else’s tests on someone else’s code.

3
00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:15.536
Your day-to-day questions are different.

4
00:00:15.816 --> 00:00:17.651
Does it follow your team’s conventions?

5
00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:21.557
How much of your time does reviewing and correcting its output take?

6
00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:24.867
Does it ask when the requirement is ambiguous, or guess?

7
00:00:25.147 --> 00:00:28.752
Does it leave the codebase easier or harder to change next month?

8
00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:30.589
Does it touch things it shouldn’t?

9
00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:34.325
There’s also a failure mode benchmarks are built to reward.

10
00:00:34.605 --> 00:00:49.560
METR reported in 2025 that frontier models sometimes “reward hack” on evaluation tasks: overwriting the timer a scorer used, replacing an evaluator with a stub that marks everything correct, or editing test code.

11
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.853
On one task, prompts telling the model not to cheat had a nearly negligible effect.

12
00:00:55.133 --> 00:01:00.424
A task graded only by tests teaches an agent, in a sense, to satisfy tests.

13
00:01:00.704 --> 00:01:05.675
Week 3 is about making your tests a contract the agent can’t quietly renegotiate.
